-# CONVLSTM-WEAR-PROGNOSTICS
-> **Category:** RUL Prognostics
+# ConvLSTM Wear Prognostics
 
-## Description
-Convolutional LSTM extracting joint spatiotemporal representations for continuous mechanical degradation tracking.
+> **Category:** RUL Prognostics  
+> **Platform adapter:** `m02-convlstm-wear`  
+> **Status:** partial (repo expects 64x64 wear maps; platform builds channel-recurrence images)  
+> **Collection position:** model **04** of 25 (alphabetical)
 
-**Model 4** extends the original scaffold (2-layer ConvLSTM) to a **4-layer stack** for deeper temporal-hierarchical wear feature extraction:
+<!-- Generated from deployment/models.catalog.json - edit the catalog, not this file. -->
 
-```
-Input (10, 16, 16, 1)   # 10-frame window of 16x16 wear maps
-  └─ ConvLSTM2D(32) + BatchNorm     # fine spatiotemporal features
-  └─ ConvLSTM2D(48) + BatchNorm     # mid-level degradation patterns
-  └─ ConvLSTM2D(48) + BatchNorm     # global wear-rate representation
-  └─ ConvLSTM2D(32) + BatchNorm     # regression-ready summary
-  └─ Flatten → Dense(64, relu) → Dense(1)   # normalized RUL output
-```
+A ConvLSTM2D stack that carries a two-dimensional wear field forward in time, keeping spatial structure that a plain LSTM would flatten away. It is the collection's spatiotemporal degradation tracker - and its bluntest example of how a research assumption meets a data reality.
+
+## How this page fits the collection
+
+The WT-PM collection numbers its 25 repositories **alphabetically 01-25** - that is the number on this page - and gives each one a **task-based adapter id** (`m02-convlstm-wear`) used by the platform registry, the `wt-pm` CLI and the fusion engine. Both are printed on every page so the ecosystem graph and the registry can be read against each other.
+
+- Repository: <https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-convlstm-wear-prognostics>
+- Landing page: <https://rajaram-2005.github.io/wt-pm-convlstm-wear-prognostics/>
+- Ecosystem graph: <https://rajaram-2005.github.io/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/ecosystem.html>
+- Platform architecture: <https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/blob/main/ARCHITECTURE.md>
+- Per-model table: <https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/blob/main/platform/docs/MODELS.md>
+
+## Model
+
+- `ConvLSTM2D model definition in model.py`
+- `Platform adapter builds recurrence images from the 12-channel window`
+
+| Block | Detail |
+|---|---|
+| Input | Upstream expects (10, 64, 64, 1) wear maps - a sequence of ten 64x64 frames |
+| Recurrent convolution | ConvLSTM2D cells replace the dense gates of an LSTM with convolutions, so hidden state keeps a spatial extent |
+| Head | Regression onto remaining useful life |
+
+## Advanced concepts
+
+The ideas this model actually leans on - stated with their failure modes, not as marketing.
+
+### Convolutional gates
+
+Every gate in a ConvLSTM is itself a convolution, so the cell learns where to remember as well as when. That is what makes it suitable for a degradation field, and what makes it 10-100x more expensive than a dense recurrent layer.
+
+### The missing 64x64 tensor
+
+No data source in the collection produces wear maps. Rather than silently fabricating them, the adapter builds 16x16 channel-recurrence images and the model is marked partial. The honest statement is: the recurrence works, the upstream spatial claim is untested.
+
+### RUL uncertainty is not optional
+
+A point estimate of remaining life is almost useless for planning without an interval. This adapter returns rul_hours only; the probabilistic statement comes from m16, which fuses these observations into a posterior.
+
+## Platform contract
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Input (adapter view) | windows -> 16x16 channel-recurrence images (the platform's substitute for unavailable wear maps) |
+| Output (`WTDataSchema`) | rul_hours |
+| Record format | `wt-pm.platform.v1` |
+| Typical latency | 15000 ms |
+| Deployment targets | cloud |
+| Resource requirements | tensorflow |
+| Fallback | `m17-mlp-rul` |
+| Subsystem focus | whole turbine |
+| Hermes tool | `rul` |
 
-- **Optimizer:** Adam (1e-3), MSE loss, MAE tracked
-- **Callbacks:** EarlyStopping (patience 3, restore best) + ReduceLROnPlateau
-- **Data:** synthetic accelerated-wear trajectory rendered as 2-D wear maps; sliding windows (stride 2) with **chronological** train/val/test split (15% / 20%) to prevent time leakage
+Records follow the shared `WTDataSchema` (`wt-pm.platform.v1`): `timestamp`, `turbine_id` and `model_id` are required; every other field is optional, so a model fills in only what it honestly produces and the fusion layer can tell absence from a zero.
 
-## Structure
+## Platform layers
+
+| Layer | What this model does there |
+|---|---|
+| **Fusion** | One of the RUL observations consumed by the m16 particle filter |
+| **Evaluation** | Scored with rul_metrics (MAE / RMSE / asymmetric penalty), not with classification metrics |
+| **Hermes** | Feeds the rul tool alongside m15 states and m17 baseline |
+| **Fallback** | Falls back to m17-mlp-rul when TensorFlow is unavailable - the platform never fails a question because a heavy model is missing |
+
+Layers are described once, in the catalog, and referenced by every model that participates in them: fusion, calibrated uncertainty, drift and data trust, explainability, physics constraints, the safety gate, the evaluation protocol and the Hermes agent.
+
