+<h1>ConvLSTM Wear Prognostics</h1>
+<p class="lede">A ConvLSTM2D stack that carries a two-dimensional wear field forward in time, keeping spatial structure that a plain LSTM would flatten away. It is the collection's spatiotemporal degradation tracker - and its bluntest example of how a research assumption meets a data reality.</p>
+<div class="cta">
+<a class="btn" href="https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-convlstm-wear-prognostics">View on GitHub</a>
+<a class="btn ghost" href="https://rajaram-2005.github.io/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/ecosystem.html">25-model ecosystem graph</a>
+<a class="btn ghost" href="https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/blob/main/ARCHITECTURE.md">Platform architecture</a>
+</div>
+</header>
+<p class="note">This page is generated from <code>deployment/models.catalog.json</code> in the reference repository, together with the other 24 model pages, so all 25 agree on numbering, contract and status.</p>
+<h2>Model</h2>
+<ul>
+<li><code>ConvLSTM2D model definition in model.py</code></li>
+<li><code>Platform adapter builds recurrence images from the 12-channel window</code></li>
+</ul>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Input</th><td>Upstream expects (10, 64, 64, 1) wear maps - a sequence of ten 64x64 frames</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Recurrent convolution</th><td>ConvLSTM2D cells replace the dense gates of an LSTM with convolutions, so hidden state keeps a spatial extent</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Head</th><td>Regression onto remaining useful life</td></tr>
+</table>
+<h2>Advanced concepts</h2>
+<p class="note">The ideas this model actually leans on, each stated with its failure mode.</p>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>Convolutional gates</h3>
+<p>Every gate in a ConvLSTM is itself a convolution, so the cell learns where to remember as well as when. That is what makes it suitable for a degradation field, and what makes it 10-100x more expensive than a dense recurrent layer.</p>
+</div>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>The missing 64x64 tensor</h3>
+<p>No data source in the collection produces wear maps. Rather than silently fabricating them, the adapter builds 16x16 channel-recurrence images and the model is marked partial. The honest statement is: the recurrence works, the upstream spatial claim is untested.</p>
+</div>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>RUL uncertainty is not optional</h3>
+<p>A point estimate of remaining life is almost useless for planning without an interval. This adapter returns rul_hours only; the probabilistic statement comes from m16, which fuses these observations into a posterior.</p>
+</div>
+<h2>Platform contract</h2>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Input (adapter view)</th><td>windows -&gt; 16x16 channel-recurrence images (the platform's substitute for unavailable wear maps)</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Output</th><td>rul_hours</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Record format</th><td><code>wt-pm.platform.v1</code></td></tr>
+<tr><th>Typical latency</th><td>15000 ms</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Deployment targets</th><td>cloud</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Requirements</th><td>tensorflow</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Fallback</th><td>m17-mlp-rul</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Subsystem focus</th><td>whole turbine</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Hermes tool</th><td><code>rul</code></td></tr>
+</table>
+<p class="note">Records follow the shared <code>WTDataSchema</code> (<code>wt-pm.platform.v1</code>). Only <code>timestamp</code>, <code>turbine_id</code> and <code>model_id</code> are required; absence is distinguishable from a zero.</p>
+<h2>Platform layers</h2>
+<table>
+<tr><th>Fusion</th><td>One of the RUL observations consumed by the m16 particle filter</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Evaluation</th><td>Scored with rul_metrics (MAE / RMSE / asymmetric penalty), not with classification metrics</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Hermes</th><td>Feeds the rul tool alongside m15 states and m17 baseline</td></tr>
+<tr><th>Fallback</th><td>Falls back to m17-mlp-rul when TensorFlow is unavailable - the platform never fails a question because a heavy model is missing</td></tr>
+</table>
+<h2>How it connects to the other 24</h2>
+<ul class="edges">
+<li><b>01. 1D-CNN Bearing Vibration</b> <span class="note">(m01-1dcnn-bearing)</span> &rarr; bearing fault labels drive wear tracking</li>
+<li><b>05. DBN Feature Extraction</b> <span class="note">(m09-dbn-features)</span> &rarr; DBN feature hierarchy for the wear model</li>
+</ul>
+<h2>Honest limits</h2><ul class="limits">
+<li>Partial: upstream 64x64 wear-map contract is not satisfiable with available data</li>
+<li>15 s typical latency - cloud only, never in the safety path</li>
+</ul>
+<p class="note">Fidelity: rung 1 of the fidelity ladder - simulator data, surrogate waveforms where stated, proxy RUL targets, simulator fault labels. Comparable within this collection, not field-validated turbine control.</p>
